#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070019 :tutorial/content "When an equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1\\. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & =3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3.$ We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3,$ where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ef92091-e041-5715-ac4e-75fa146c4cea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8692 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
